> > but `<?marker name=">"?>` could happen too ... or `<!marker name=">">` ... are these equally problematic? I see the processing instruction _less_ problematic as it requires `?>` to close itself ... would `name="?>"` break the world though?
> 
> The HTML parser needs to unconditionally close it at the first `>`.

Right, to avoid compat issues with browsers that don't support it. The end of the PI has to be in the same place as the end of a bogus comment today.

@noamr you sent me here from the parsing PR (https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12118#issuecomment-3998898012)

I'm not sure this feature is the relevant one though. Yes, this feature wants to describe ranges of node, but what should a library do if it also wants to describe ranges with it's own PIs?

What I was suggesting in https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12118#issuecomment-3998873031 was the possibly of allowing arbitrary start/end PI pairs. ie, something like `<?start-lit-part>...<?end-lit-part>`.
